# Soils and Natural Vegetation: India's Living Cover

> Soil takes centuries to form and only seasons to lose. This lesson
> classifies India's eight soil groups, the erosion that threatens them,
> and the natural vegetation they support.

## 1. How Soil Forms

[definition]
**Soil** is the loose surface layer in which plants root, made of
mineral grains, organic matter (humus), water, and air. It is built by
the interplay of five factors over time: **parent rock, climate,
relief, organisms, and time**.
[/definition]

| Factor | Contribution |
| --- | --- |
| Parent rock | Mineral skeleton and chemistry |
| Climate | Speed of weathering and leaching |
| Relief | Slope drains or drowns the soil |
| Organisms | Humus, roots, and mixing burrows |
| Time | Depth and maturity of the profile |

## 2. The Eight ICAR Soil Groups

| Group | Home | Traits | Suited crops |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Alluvial (~43% of area) | Ganga–Brahmaputra plains, coasts | Deep, fertile, renewed by floods | Rice, wheat, sugarcane, jute |
| Black (regur) | Deccan Trap lava country | Clay-rich, cracks in summer, holds moisture | Cotton, soybean, jowar |
| Red | Eastern and southern Deccan | Iron-stained, porous, low fertility | Millets, pulses, groundnut |
| Laterite | High-rainfall Ghats and hills | Heavily leached, hardens on exposure | Tea, coffee, cashew, rubber |
| Arid | Rajasthan desert, Kutch | Sandy, kankar-rich, low humus | Bajra and hardy pulses |
| Forest / mountain | Himalayan slopes | Thin, immature, acidic in conifer tracts | Plantation and terrace crops |
| Saline–alkaline | Rann of Kutch, over-irrigated plains | Salt crusts (reh, kallar) starve roots | Reclaimed only with gypsum |
| Peaty / marshy | Kerala coast, deltas | Waterlogged, humus-rich, black | Paddy after drainage |

[definition]
In the alluvial plains, **khadar** is the fresh, fertile silt of
low-lying flood tracts, while **bhangar** is the older, higher alluvium
studded with lime nodules called **kankar**.
[/definition]

[tip]
Black soil's talent for holding monsoon moisture is why the Deccan can
grow cotton without much irrigation — and why the same soil turns
into sticky, plough-resistant clay in a wet week.
[/tip]

## 3. Soil Erosion and Conservation

| Form | What happens | Classic example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Gully erosion | Runoff carves deep channels | Chambal ravines |
| Sheet erosion | Thin layers wash evenly off slopes | Degraded red-soil uplands |
| Wind erosion | Dust and sand blow away | Rajasthan's sandy margins |

Conservation toolkit: **contour ploughing** (furrows across the slope),
**terrace farming** (steps on steep hills), **shelterbelts** (tree rows
against the wind), **strip cropping**, check dams, and **afforestation**.

[warning]
India loses billions of tonnes of topsoil every year — erosion works
quietly, invisibly, and every monsoon, unlike a flood that announces
itself once.
[/warning]

## 4. Types of Natural Vegetation

@diagram tree id="india-vegetation-types" layout="tree" aria="India's natural vegetation types"
  "Natural vegetation" -> "Tropical evergreen"
  "Natural vegetation" -> "Tropical deciduous"
  "Natural vegetation" -> "Thorn and scrub"
  "Natural vegetation" -> Montane
  "Natural vegetation" -> Mangrove
@/diagram

| Type | Rainfall | Where | Signature trees |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Tropical evergreen | Over 200 cm | Western Ghats, north-east, Andamans | Rosewood, ebony, mahogany, rubber |
| Tropical deciduous | 70–200 cm | Most widespread type | Teak, sal, sandalwood — shed leaves in the dry season |
| Thorn and scrub | Under 75 cm | Rajasthan, Gujarat, leeward Deccan | Cactus, babool (acacia), khejri, date palm |
| Montane | Varies with altitude | The Himalaya | Pine, deodar, fir, spruce, birch, then alpine meadows |
| Mangrove (tidal) | — | Sundarbans delta | Sundari trees with pneumatophore breathing roots |

The **Sundarbans** — named for the sundari tree — shelter the **Royal
Bengal tiger** in a lattice of tidal mud and breathing roots.

## 5. Conserving the Wild Cover

- **Biosphere reserves** — 18 protect whole ecosystems; the **Nilgiri
  reserve (1986)** was India's first.
- **National parks** — the **Jim Corbett National Park (1936)** began
  the tradition.
- **Project Tiger (1973)** — today links dozens of tiger reserves.
- **Wildlife sanctuaries and Ramsar wetlands** — shield birds, marshes,
  and migratory stop-overs.

## 6. Quick Revision

| Question | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| Largest soil group? | Alluvial (~43% of India's area) |
| Black soil grows? | Cotton — it is 'black cotton soil' |
| Khadar is? | Fresh flood-plain alluvium |
| Chambal ravines come from? | Gully erosion |
| Most widespread vegetation? | Tropical deciduous |
| First biosphere reserve? | Nilgiri, 1986 |
| First national park? | Jim Corbett, 1936 |
